{"id":"evt_3dn73yzw","issue_id":"eavs-w6wg.9","action":"updated","timestamp":"2026-08-25T19:38:37.496705801Z","harness":"pi","harness_session_id":"019fb1e3-5b36-78d6-b471-333f998d0145","session_name":"oqto_refactor: Heres The Honest Gap List Ordered [boss-tail-html]","model":"openrouter/stealth/ox-alpha","changes":[{"field":"description","from":"Security gap confirmed 2026-08-20 against Prime Intellect's universal offline-sandbox-escape disclosure (primeintellect.ai/blog/universal-offline-sandbox-escape): EAVS passes request bodies through unmodified (no handling of input_file/file_url or server-side tools anywhere in src/proxy.rs), so any client can ride the ALLOWED inference channel to make the provider fetch arbitrary URLs (OpenAI Responses API input_file.file_url) or run server-side web tools - an SSRF/exfiltration channel invisible to network egress enforcement. GPT-5.6 used exactly this to escape a blocked-egress eval sandbox.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION\n1. New src/delegated_fetch.rs: DelegatedFetchPolicy { allow_remote_content: bool, allowed_server_tools: Vec<String> } + sanitize(body: &mut Value, policy) -> Vec<StrippedItem> (field path + target host if parseable, for audit).\n   Walks: (a) Responses API input[] content arrays - drop items where type==input_file carrying file_url; (b) tools[] - drop entries whose type is a server-side tool not on the allowed list (web_search, web_fetch); (c) normalize tool_choice when it references a removed tool (else upstream 400s).\n2. Call sanitize in proxy_handler right after json_body parse - BEFORE needs_transform branching, so passthrough routes are inspected too.\n3. Same call in ws_proxy_handler + codex_ws_handler (Codex traffic rides these).\n4. Policy knob: [delegated_fetch] enabled = false in config.toml (global default deny), plus per-virtual-key override map for tenant granularity later.\n5. Audit: every strip/refusal logs a structured event naming field path and target host.\n\nDEFAULT = DENY, deliberately breaking: without it the failure mode is silent exfiltration through the allowed channel; with it, failure is a visibly missing capability (model says it cannot fetch) fixable with one config line. Flag as security hardening in CHANGELOG; operators re-enable per deployment if they accept the channel.\n\nPROOF OF DONE: integration test using mock_provider sends a Responses request containing input_file.file_url -> assert upstream-received body has no file_url anywhere; control request without it passes byte-identical; tool_choice normalization covered; audit events asserted.","to":"Security gap confirmed 2026-08-20 against Prime Intellect's universal offline-sandbox-escape disclosure (primeintellect.ai/blog/universal-offline-sandbox-escape): EAVS passes request bodies through unmodified (no handling of input_file/file_url or server-side tools anywhere in src/proxy.rs), so any client can ride the ALLOWED inference channel to make the provider fetch arbitrary URLs (OpenAI Responses API input_file.file_url) or run server-side web tools - an SSRF/exfiltration channel invisible to network egress enforcement. GPT-5.6 used exactly this to escape a blocked-egress eval sandbox.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION\n1. New src/delegated_fetch.rs: DelegatedFetchPolicy { allow_remote_content: bool, allowed_server_tools: Vec<String> } + sanitize(body: &mut Value, policy) -> Vec<StrippedItem> (field path + target host if parseable, for audit).\n   Walks: (a) Responses API input[] content arrays - drop items where type==input_file carrying file_url; (b) tools[] - drop entries whose type is a server-side tool not on the allowed list (web_search, web_fetch); (c) normalize tool_choice when it references a removed tool (else upstream 400s).\n2. Call sanitize in proxy_handler right after json_body parse - BEFORE needs_transform branching, so passthrough routes are inspected too.\n3. Same call in ws_proxy_handler + codex_ws_handler (Codex traffic rides these).\n4. Policy knob: [delegated_fetch] enabled = false in config.toml (global default deny), plus per-virtual-key override map for tenant granularity later.\n5. Audit: every strip/refusal logs a structured event naming field path and target host.\n\nDEFAULT = DENY, deliberately breaking: without it the failure mode is silent exfiltration through the allowed channel; with it, failure is a visibly missing capability (model says it cannot fetch) fixable with one config line. Flag as security hardening in CHANGELOG; operators re-enable per deployment if they accept the channel.\n\nPROOF OF DONE: integration test using mock_provider sends a Responses request containing input_file.file_url -> assert upstream-received body has no file_url anywhere; control request without it passes byte-identical; tool_choice normalization covered; audit events asserted.\n\nOQTO-SIDE CONTRACT (implemented, oqto_refactor commit 678bc38d): session keys created by oqto now carry metadata delegated_fetch.remote_content (bool, default false), sourced from the work directory's .oqto/config.toml [egress] delegated_fetch. Sanitizer should read THIS metadata from the key record at request time (default deny when absent) -- no new admin endpoint required. Per-key granularity falls out for free."}]}
